<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Balasinor State</h1><p class="st-meta">1758 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Balasinor</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Babi Pathan Nawabi state of central Gujarat, now best known for the dinosaur fossils found on its former lands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Balasinor was founded around 1758 by a member of the Babi family, the Pathan dynasty that also held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh State</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/radhanpur-state">Radhanpur State</a>. It survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> period by paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Nawabs governed a small agricultural territory as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rewa-kantha-agency">Rewa Kantha Agency</a>. Little is recorded about its internal politics. In 1981 large deposits of dinosaur bones and eggs were found at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raiyoli">Raiyoli</a>, now a fossil park promoted by members of the former ruling family.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Balasinor was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> in 1948 and has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> since 1960; it lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahisagar-district">Mahisagar district</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/balasinor-state.jpg" alt="Balasinor State" width="320" height="284"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balasinor_princely_state_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1758 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Balasinor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Sunni Islam (ruling house)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Balasinor, Wadasinor</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1853&amp;focus=balasinor-state" aria-label="Balasinor State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6640 1331 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Balasinor State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6960 1587L7364 1601M6960 1587L7364 1602M6960 1587L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6960 1587h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6960 1587h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Balasinor. <a href="/atlas?year=1853&amp;focus=balasinor-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
